<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#parthian-shot">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parthian shot</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Parthian shot was a mounted archery tactic in which a horse archer, real or feigning retreat, turned in the saddle to shoot at pursuing enemies. It was characteristic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>'s cavalry and of other steppe peoples. The phrase survives in English, often as parting shot, for a final remark made on leaving.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parthian-shot.jpg" alt="Parthian shot" width="311" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OttomanHorseArcher.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
